'''Costaceae''', known as the ''Costus'' family or '''spiral gingers''', is a [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] of [[pantropical]] [[Monocotyledon|monocots]]. It belongs to the [[Order (biology)|order]] [[Zingiberales]], which contains [[Horticulture|horticulturally]] and [[Commerce|economically]] important [[plant]]s such as the [[banana]] (Musaceae), [[Strelitzia reginae|bird-of-paradise]] (Strelitziaceae), and edible [[ginger]] (Zingiberaceae). The seven [[genera]] in Costaceae together contain about 143 known [[species]]<ref name="Christenhusz-Byng2016">{{cite journal |author1=Christenhusz, M. J. M. |author2=Byng, J. W. | year = 2016 | title = The number of known plants species in the world and its annual increase | journal = Phytotaxa | volume = 261 | pages = 201–217 | url = http://biotaxa.org/Phytotaxa/article/download/phytotaxa.261.3.1/20598 | doi = 10.11646/phytotaxa.261.3.1 | issue = 3 | publisher = Magnolia Press | doi-access = free }}</ref> (1 in ''Monocostus'', 2 in ''Dimerocostus'', 16 in ''Tapeinochilos'', 2 in ''Paracostus'', {{circa}} 8 in ''Chamaecostus'', c. 5 in ''Hellenia'', and c. 80 in ''Costus'').<ref name=specht2006>Chelsea D. Specht & Dennis Wm. Stevenson. 2006. "A new phylogeny-based generic classification of Costaceae (Zingiberales)".''Taxon'' '''55'''(1):153-163. (See ''External links'' below).</ref> They are [[Indigenous (ecology)|native]] to [[tropical climate]]s of [[Asia]], [[Africa]], [[Central America]], and [[South America]]. Several species are frequently found in [[Gardening|cultivation]].{{citation needed|date=September 2015}}

== Description ==
The [[Simple leaf|simple]] [[Leaf|leaves]] are [[Entire leaf|entire]] and spirally [[Phyllotaxis|arranged]], with those toward the base of the [[Plant stem|stem]] usually bladeless. Leaf bases have a closed [[Leaf sheath|sheath]] with a [[ligule]], or projection at the top of the sheath.

Costaceae is different from the other families of Zingiberales in that its species have 5 [[Connation|fused]] [[staminode]]s, rather than 2 or 3, and the Costaceae contain no [[Aroma compound|aromatic]] [[oil]]s. The fused infertile [[stamen]]s form a large petaloid [[Labellum (botany)|labellum]] that often functions to attract [[pollinator]]s. The [[flower]]s are solitary in ''Monocostus''. In the other genera, the flowers are borne in a terminal [[Spike (botany)|spike]] that ranges from elongate to nearly [[Glossary of botanical terms#capitate|capitate]]. Each flower is [[Glossary of botanical terms#subtend|subtended]] by a large [[bract]]. The [[fruit]] is a [[berry (botany)|berry]] or [[Capsule (fruit)|capsule]]. The [[rhizome]] is fleshy with [[tuber]]ous roots.

